# Phase 6 - reviewed false positives in the Verifiable Intent proof evidence.
#
# The proof writes one evidence file per case under
# docs/proofs/mastercard-vi/evidence/. Three of its JSON fields hold a
# long, high-entropy value beside a field name containing the word key,
# which is the shape generic-api-key matches. None of the three is a key.
# Each is a one-way digest computed FROM public material:
#
#   * one is the SHA-256 of the Ed25519 receipt verifying key. The
#     verifying key is the public half; it is published in the same file
#     and the digest recomputes from it exactly.
#   * two are RFC 7638 JWK thumbprints of the agent's public P-256 JWK,
#     which likewise recompute from the public key alone.
#
# A digest of a public value discloses nothing the file does not already
# state outright, and none of them can be presented to anything.
#
# Verified before these entries were added, against the exact committed
# bytes: no RFC 7517 private JWK member appears anywhere in the pack; the
# only JWK emitted carries crv/kty/x/y and no private member; and a
# negative search for all five proof private keys - as raw scalars, PKCS8
# and SEC1 DER, and PEM, in seven encodings, 140 needles - found nothing.
# No PEM private-key header, database DSN, bearer value or JWT-shaped
# string is present either. The generator serialises public material only.
#
# The proof keys are themselves derived from published labels, so even a
# genuine disclosure would expose nothing operational. That is not the
# reason these are ignored; the reason is that they are public digests.
#
# Scoped to exact fingerprints, so a new occurrence anywhere - including a
# new evidence file, or a real credential added to an existing one - still
# fails the gate.
#
# NOTE: as elsewhere in this file, do not write an example value next to
# any of those field names in a comment. That reproduces the pattern here
# and the gate flags this file.
